```python
def naive_search(text, pattern):
    n, m = len(text), len(pattern)
    matches = []
    for i in range(n - m + 1):
        j = 0
        while j < m and text[i + j] == pattern[j]:
            j += 1
        if j == m:
            matches.append(i)
    return matches
```

Every outer iteration restarts `j` at 0 — no memory of any previous comparison carries forward. On `text = "aaaaaaaaab"`, `pattern = "aaab"`, almost every starting position matches 3 characters before failing on the 4th, and none of that partial progress is ever reused.

```python
def kmp_search(text, pattern):
    n, m = len(text), len(pattern)
    if m == 0:
        return []
    lps = build_lps(pattern)
    matches = []
    i = j = 0
    while i < n:
        if text[i] == pattern[j]:
            i += 1
            j += 1
            if j == m:
                matches.append(i - j)
                j = lps[j - 1]        # look for the next match, don't stop here
        elif j > 0:
            j = lps[j - 1]            # jump using the failure array
        else:
            i += 1                    # no progress to salvage, advance i
    return matches
```

On a full match (`j == m`), the code doesn't reset `j` to 0 — it also falls back to `lps[j-1]`, which correctly handles overlapping matches (e.g. finding `"aa"` inside `"aaa"` at both position 0 and 1).
